@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Foundations-Grid--Layout a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Foundations: Grid & Layout";
    ds:summary "This document covers the horizontal grid system: column counts, column spans, gutters, outer margins, and layout strategy per tier.\n\n- Column structure and bisection rule\n- Gutter and outer margin values per tier and breakpoint\n- Viewport-based vs container-based grid contexts\n- Drawer and panel sizing (applications)\n- Nested grid alignment\n- When to use the grid vs other layout primitives";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "**Status:** Final  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Last updated:** 2026-04-24\n\n\n\n## Table of contents\n\n1. [Glossary](#glossary)\n2. [Problem statement and scope](#1-problem-statement-and-scope)\n3. [Core principles (universal)](#2-core-principles)\n4. [Editorial and marketing sites](#3-editorial-and-marketing-sites)\n5. [Documentation](#4-documentation)\n6. [Applications](#5-applications)\n7. [Responsive reflow](#6-responsive-reflow)\n8. [When to use the grid](#7-when-to-use-the-grid)\n9. [Form layouts](#8-form-layouts)\n10. [Future work](#9-future-work)\n\n\n\n## Glossary\n\nTable 21\n\n\n\n## 1. Problem statement and scope\n\n### 1.1 The problem\n\nOur design system serves three tiers:\n\n- [Apps](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-KdW0T6RE3B/_rui-mWBJDa-kOF) **:** Application UIs – dashboards, forms, settings pages, admin panels (MAAS, Landscape, Launchpad, Snapcraft, support portal, and ~50 other products)\n- [Sites](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-KdW0T6RE3B/_rui-T4g_fD6A91) : Editorial / marketing sites – [ubuntu.com](http://ubuntu.com), [canonical.com](http://canonical.com), blog content\n- [Documentation](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-KdW0T6RE3B/_rui-RHPOgKqlyQ)  **Docs:** Product documentation\n\nThese tiers have different grid needs. Application UIs require multiple concurrent grid contexts (main content alongside drawers, panels, and toolbars) with fluid, edge-to-edge layouts. Editorial and marketing sites use a single centered grid with a maximum content width and wider gutters that give editorial content more breathing room. Documentation shares the editorial grid model but typically uses narrower content columns with side navigation.\n\nA single grid configuration does not serve all three well. This document defines the shared column system and the per-tier rules built on top of it.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nThis specification covers the horizontal grid system: column counts, column spans, gutters, outer margins, and layout strategy per tier.\n\n- Column structure and bisection rule\n- Gutter and outer margin values per tier and breakpoint\n- Viewport-based vs container-based grid contexts\n- Drawer and panel sizing (applications)\n- Nested grid alignment\n- When to use the grid vs other layout primitives\n\nOut of scope:\n\n- Vertical spacing and baseline grid (see the [spacing specification](https://coda.io/d/Docs_dTaVNZxkPrA/Spacing-architecture-0-4_su8_Tyhn#Table-11_turSlbF2))\n- Typography specification (referenced where relevant)\n- Implementation details (CSS architecture, component APIs, tooling)\n\n### 1.3 Goals\n\n1. **Predictability:** Grid behaviour is deterministic and learnable across all tiers\n2. **Visual coherence:** Shared column counts and bisection rule produce consistent keylines everywhere\n3. **Tier fit:** Each tier uses the grid model best suited to it\n4. **Composability:** Components placed on the grid in one tier work correctly when reused in another\n\n\n\n## 2. Core principles\n\nThese apply universally across all three tiers.\n\n### 2.1 Column counts\n\nColumn counts are determined by **container width** (applications) or **viewport width** (sites and documentation):\n\nTable 22\n\nThe two 8-column ranges are intentionally separate. Both use the same column count, but the breakpoint between them gives components a second threshold at which to switch layout – for example from stacked to side-by-side – within the mid-range.\n\n**Rationale for 240px minimum**: This is our current default minimum aside width, below which content truncation becomes excessive. It accommodates the minimum comfortable width for form fields, navigation items, and side panels.\n\n**Rationale for column counts**: We intentionally use only 4, 8, or 16 columns to create a structure where keylines either bisect spaces or follow parent keylines. This avoids the awkward offset-by-1 alignment issues that occur with 12-column grids (which permit thirds), and the column rivers that appear when the browser is left to choose a count freely. \n\n\n\n*Order emerging from restraint: 8 column grid with disciplined subdivision on the right compared to a 12 column grid with no restrictions.*\n\n### 2.2 Column span rule\n\nValid spans are power-of-2 fractions of the current column count – half, quarter, or eighth:\n\nTable 23\n\nThis allows uniform rows (e.g. four 2-column-wide cards on an 8-column grid) and the standard 50/50 or 25/75 two-part splits. It disallows thirds and any other non-bisecting division – the same principle that drove the move from 12-column to 8-column grids across the Canonical web portfolio.\n\n### 2.3 Gutter widths and outer margins\n\nGutters and outer margins are set by the **global viewport breakpoint**, not by container width. The tiers share the same values at small viewports but diverge at larger sizes – editorial sites and documentation use wider gutters for the more generous rhythm that content-heavy pages need.\n\n**Sites and documentation:**\n\nTable 24\n\n**Applications:**\n\nTable 25\n\nThe difference is at Large and X-Large: sites and documentation step up to 2rem (32px) gutters, while applications stay at 1.5rem (24px). Application interfaces are denser – tighter gutters give more room to data-heavy content, panels, and multi-column controls. Sites have more breathing room by design, so the wider gutter matches the editorial rhythm.\n\n### 2.4 Nested grids\n\n**All nested grids follow their parent container’s grid structure.** When a component establishes a grid within a parent grid cell, columns can only start at parent column offsets. This creates consistent vertical keylines throughout the interface.\n\nNo column is allowed to start anywhere except at parent column offsets. The grid subdivides through bisection (octree pattern), maintaining alignment at every nesting level.\n\n\n\n\n\n## 3. Editorial and marketing sites\n\nEditorial and marketing sites ([ubuntu.com](http://ubuntu.com), [canonical.com](http://canonical.com), blog content) use a single, viewport-based grid.\n\n### 3.1 Viewport-based grid\n\nThe full page width minus outer margins forms one grid context. There are no independent sub-grids for drawers or panels – the viewport is the only trigger for column count changes. Column count switches at the standard breakpoint thresholds (see §2.1).\n\n### 3.2 Centered, max-width layout\n\nContent is centered within the viewport and constrained to a maximum width of 80rem. At viewports wider than the max-width, the outer margins grow to absorb the excess space. This is the opposite of application layouts, which fill all available space edge-to-edge.\n\n\n\n### 3.3 Responsive reflow\n\nReflow on sites is driven by **viewport breakpoints**. When the viewport crosses a breakpoint threshold, column count and gutter width update together. There are no panels to trigger mid-viewport reflow – the only trigger is the viewport itself resizing.\n\n\n\n## 4. Documentation\n\nDocumentation follows the same grid model as editorial/marketing sites – viewport-based, centered, same gutter values – but uses a three-region layout:\n\n- **Left: site navigation** – persistent side navigation across all documentation pages. Collapses into a drawer at smaller breakpoints.\n- **Centre: content body** – the article or page content.\n- **Right: on-page table of contents** – anchor links to headings within the current page. At smaller breakpoints this stacks directly below the page’s h1 heading.\n\nEach region occupies a fixed column span on the grid. The content area respects the grid boundaries throughout.\n\n\n\n[example: documentation layout with side navigation and content area across breakpoints]\n\n\n\n## 5. Applications\n\nApplication UIs (for example, MAAS, LXD, Landscape) require multiple concurrent grid contexts and fluid, edge-to-edge layouts.\n\n\n\n### 5.1 Container-based grids\n\nEach major layout region (main content, panels/drawers, toolbars) can establish its own grid context based on its available width. **Panels** are layout zones that resize the main content (e.g., desktop navigation), while **drawers** overlay the main content without changing its size. This allows:\n\n- Consistent layouts regardless of panel positioning\n- Predictable behavior when drawers or panels are resized, hidden, or overlayed\n- Reusable components that adapt to their container\n\n**Key principle**: A drawer at any intrinsic width uses the gutters defined by the **global viewport breakpoint**, not by its own width. For example, a 320px side drawer displayed on a 1800px screen uses 1.5rem (24px) gutters (the application large-breakpoint value), not 1rem (16px). This ensures visual consistency across all drawers and the main area at the same screen size.\n\n### 5.2 Fluid layouts\n\n\n\nApplications use fluid grids that expand to fill available space. Unlike editorial sites, applications should not:\n\n- Have a maximum content width that leaves whitespace at large screens\n- Use centered positioning for the main layout container\n\nFluid grids ensure consistent, edge-to-edge space utilisation across application views and window sizes.\n\nThis spec proposes **container queries** to switch column counts: when a container crosses a defined width threshold, its column count explicitly switches to 4, 8, or 16. Gutter and outer margin values are controlled separately by the global viewport breakpoint (see §2.3). This combination gives explicit, predictable control over both column structure and spacing at every screen size.\n\nNote: We’re currently experimenting with an alternative, “intrinsic” grid approach that uses `auto-fill`, `auto-fit`, or `minmax()` to let the browser determine column count dynamically. The main rationale for this approach is support for predominantly left-aligned layouts: as the browser adds columns to fill available width, the grid naturally grows from the left edge, which makes centered compositions difficult and in practice rules them out. That same behavior creates an interesting opportunity for controlled breakout patterns, where a primary content area can remain on an 8-column reading width while additional columns are used for wider elements such as card grids. This should be used deliberately and with care, because it relaxes some of the explicit 4/8/16-column guardrails this spec is designed to preserve and can introduce column counts outside the canonical set. Container queries remain the recommended implementation mechanism whenever the design depends on one of the three defined column counts; intrinsic behavior is better treated as a deliberate exception for left-aligned and breakout use cases, not the default.\n\n### 5.3 Drawers vs panels\n\nWe distinguish between two types of layout regions:\n\n- **Drawers** overlap the main area without changing its size. Drawers sit on top of the main content without changing its size.\n- **Panels** exist in the same plane as the main area and cause the main area to resize.\n\nApplication layouts have one panel slot per browser window edge. That slot can be subdivided internally – for example into a narrow icon rail and a wider navigation list – but from the grid’s perspective it is a single region with a single width. The main content area occupies the remaining space and establishes its own grid context based on its available width.\n\n### 5.4 Panel/Drawer width constraints\n\nEach drawer type defines minimum and maximum widths. When placed in application landmark regions (`<aside>`, `<main>`, etc.), these internally defined constraints apply:\n\nTable 26\n\n**All applications must use these standardised widths on load.** Inconsistent drawer widths have been identified as a recurring problem across our product suite. If additional flexibility is needed, implement user-adjustable resizing within the min/max constraints above – but the default loaded state must always use the standard small/medium/large values.\n\nTable 9\n\n[Examples of 3 panel/drawer widths. A high contrast border in examples 1 and 2 serves as a resize affordance]\n\n### 5.5 Tile grid\n\nDashboard tile layouts are a common use case. We have explored an approach where component padding (on cards and similar elements) is absorbed into the grid gutters, increasing the proportion of space available for content. This is out of scope for the current spec – see [Future work](#9-future-work) for details and examples. For now, align cards to the grid.\n\n\n\n## 6. Responsive reflow\n\nContainer queries (applications) and viewport breakpoints (sites and documentation) drive all layout transitions. Each grid context has a defined layout for every width bracket – the browser moves between those defined states automatically as the width changes. The product designer’s job is to specify the layout at each bracket; the browser handles transitions between them.\n\n### 6.1 Panel-triggered reflow (applications)\n\nWhen a panel opens or is resized, the main area’s available width shrinks. If that crosses a container query threshold, the main area switches to the corresponding column layout automatically – no additional logic required. The same applies to panels themselves: a panel that narrows past a threshold switches to its own narrower layout. This is container queries working as intended, not a special case.\n\n### 6.2 Narrowing strategy (all tiers)\n\nWhen a grid container becomes too narrow to accommodate its current layout:\n\n1. **First**: Move to the narrower width bracket, which may reduce column count and spans (e.g., switch from an 8-column layout to a 4-column layout)\n2. **Then**: Switch from horizontal to vertical stacking if the narrower layout still doesn’t fit\n\nThis ensures content remains readable and functional at any width, whether the trigger is the viewport resizing (sites and documentation) or a panel opening (applications).\n\n\n\n## 7. When to use the grid\n\n### 7.1 Required\n\nThe grid **must** be used when placing blocks of content side by side. Some examples:\n\n- Placing block-level elements side-by-side (e.g., cards, form sections, data panels)\n- Creating multi-column layouts within a content area\n- Positioning elements that should align vertically across rows\n- Laying out dashboard widgets or data visualisations\n\n### 7.2 Optional\n\nThe grid is **optional** for:\n\n- **Tables**: While the table as a whole must span an exact number of columns, Internal column logic takes precedence. **Guideline**: If aligning table columns to the grid won’t hurt the content (i.e., grid column widths are suitable for the data inside), align to the grid. If alignment causes issues with data display, the table’s internal logic takes precedence.\n\n\n\n### 7.3 Not applicable\n\nThe grid is **not applicable** for:\n\n- **Inline content flows**: Tab labels, toolbar buttons, breadcrumbs – these behave like text and use natural inline layout\n- **Single-column content**: Articles, lists, side navigation – no subdivision needed\n\n### 7.4 Examples – sites and documentation\n\n**Landing page** (grid required):\n\n[example needed]\n\n**Article with side navigation** (grid required):\n\n- Sticky side nav in a narrow column span, article body in the remaining span\n- On narrow viewports the side nav stacks above or collapses\n\n\n\n### 7.5 Examples – applications\n\n\n\n**Combobox strip** (grid optional):\n\n- Labels flow inline like text (no grid needed)\n- But the combobox stri[ can be assigned a column span (e.g., “columns 4–8”)\n\n**Toolbar with left/right groups** (grid exempt):\n\n- Heading floated left\n- Right-aligned elements use CSS flexbox/float, not grid columns\n\n**Data table in main area** (grid optional):\n\n- Table has internal column logic\n- If table columns can align to grid without compromising data display: align to grid\n- If alignment causes truncation or awkward column widths: table takes precedence\n\n**Drawer** (grid required):\n\n- \n\n\n\n\n\n## 8. Form layouts\n\nForms align to grid columns based on their context:\n\n- Centered, spanning 4 columns (columns 3–6) on an 8-column grid – a half-width block in the middle of the page  \n\n- **Left-aligned** (applications): Left-aligned, starting from the first column  \n\n\n\n\n**Summary of constraints:**\n\n*Universal (all tiers):*\n\n- Column counts: 4, 8, or 16 only\n- Column spans: power-of-2 fractions of the column count (half, quarter, eighth) – no thirds or other non-bisecting splits\n- Nested grids: must align to parent column offsets\n- Gutters: At x-small and small breakpoints, gutters are universal (see the table in seciton 2.3). At larger breakpoints, gutter behavior changes, see below. \n\n*Sites and documentation:*\n\n- Grid context: single viewport-based grid\n- Gutters at large/x-large: 2rem (32px)\n- Layout: centered, max-width constrained\n\n*Applications:*\n\n- Grid context: container-based, multiple concurrent contexts\n- Gutters at large/x-large: 1.5rem (24px)\n- Layout: fluid, edge-to-edge\n- Drawer widths: defined min/max per drawer type, with standardised default widths on load\n\n\n\n## 9. Future work\n\n### Tile grid approaches\n\nDashboard views with tile-based content benefit from a specialised grid configuration. Two approaches worth exploring:\n\n- **Gutter-as-padding**: Use a 2px (or more) gap between tiles, with card padding absorbed into the gutter. This maximises content area within each tile.\n- **Half-gutter variant**: Split the gutter value between tile outer spacing and card inner padding, giving more control over the relative weight of gap vs card breathing room.\n- Future work: add examples for:\n\n[example needed: gutter-as-padding layout vs standard grid layout, side by side]  \n[example needed: half-gutter variant showing relative padding proportions]";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
